SYNOPSIS
        int update_blueprint(string|object source)
        int update_blueprint(string|object source, int|object* targets)

DESCRIPTION
        Queue one deferred blueprint update preparation. This experimental
        implementation requires USE_BLUEPRINT_UPDATE. It does not migrate
        objects: requests finish with status "failed", and a compatible
        preparation reports IMPLEMENTATION_INCOMPLETE.

        A string source must name an already loaded ordinary blueprint.
        The request captures that object and its exact program, then privately
        compiles the file at execution against its captured parent programs.
        The source blueprint participates implicitly, even with targets ({}).
        The candidate is never registered or published as a live program.

        An object source must be the actual loaded ordinary blueprint. Its
        exact program is the candidate; this mode does not compile a file or
        implicitly select the source blueprint for an update.

        Omitted targets, or integer zero, select all matching ordinary clones
        found at execution, after compiler/master callbacks. Other integers
        are invalid. An array selects only its copied, deduplicated identities.
        Every input element must be a live same-family clone or the source
        blueprint. A listed blueprint is excluded from clone counts and is
        never processed twice. An empty array selects no clones. Changes to
        the caller's array, including changes during privilege_violation(),
        do not change the captured selection. Destroyed selected clones are
        skipped at execution; unsupported surviving members fail the request.

        The master must authorize privilege_violation("update_blueprint",
        caller, source, targets). Arguments and admission are revalidated
        after this callback. Invalid arguments, denied privilege, exhausted
        identifiers, and admission limits raise before assigning an ID.
        Lightweight callers and special, virtual, replaced, shadowing or
        shadowed objects are unsupported. Pending replace_program() work in
        the same family also blocks admission or execution.

        The positive ID increases monotonically for the lifetime of the
        driver. Submission performs no compilation or migration. A request
        gets one attempt at the next periodic backend tick after the current
        stack returns, before heartbeat dispatch. Nested submissions wait
        for the following tick. Processing also runs without object heartbeats.
        Destruction of the requester cancels its pending/in-flight requests
        and removes its cached reports. Source destruction or reloading cannot
        substitute a new identity for the captured source.

        Configurable defaults are 64 pending/in-flight requests globally,
        8 per owner, 10000 selected clones, 1000000 examined objects/raw array
        entries, 1000000 processed variable slots, 64 MiB per request for
        retained preparation/report storage, and literal nesting depth 64.
        Only one pending/in-flight request may reserve a canonical family,
        including disjoint explicit selections. Clone quotas apply after
        deduplication; raw input examination has its own bound. Schema work
        and storage are shared across generations and initializer decisions.
        The byte allowance is per request, not a global byte reservation.
        Temporary compilation and LPC values also obey normal driver limits.

SEE ALSO
